"Order also helps with structure. In 日, writing the middle stroke before the bottom line lets you see the space you are dividing, and the bottom line closes the box neatly at the end. In 三, writing from the top down lets you compare each line with the one above it, so you can make the middle one short and the bottom one long.": "c88a7770bd5c",
"Practice paper helps, too. A nine-square grid divides each square into nine smaller squares, like the character 井, so you can check where each stroke begins and ends. The 田 grid and the 米 grid do the same job. Long ago, children started school by tracing red characters in a copybook that began 上大人, 孔乙己, chosen because the characters have very few strokes.": "e746b18f427a",
"Six of the rules are easy to see. Write from left to right, as in 川 (river), and from top to bottom, as in 三 (three). Write a horizontal stroke before a vertical stroke that crosses it, as in 十 (ten), and a left-falling stroke before a right-falling stroke that meets it, as in 人 (person). When a vertical stroke stands alone in the middle, like the one in 小 (small), write it first. For a box like 日 (sun), write the outside, then the inside, and close the bottom last.": "19b6d7a55023",
"Stroke order is simply the order in which the strokes of a character are written. In Taiwan, the Ministry of Education sets a standard order for thousands of characters. Its handbook of standard characters sums up the order in 17 basic rules, and its stroke-order website, first opened in 2008, now shows animations for 6,063 characters that anyone can look up.": "70ce921bf3b6",
"Stroke order is simply the order in which the strokes of a character are written. In Taiwan, the Ministry of Education sets a standard order for thousands of characters. Its handbook of standard characters sums up the order in 17 basic rules, and its stroke-order website, first opened in 2008, now shows animations for 6,063 characters that anyone can look up. Six of the rules are easy to see. Write from left to right, as in 川 (river), and from top to bottom, as in 三 (three). Write a horizontal stroke before a vertical stroke that crosses it, as in 十 (ten), and a left-falling stroke before a right-falling stroke that meets it, as in 人 (person). When a vertical stroke stands alone in the middle, like the one in 小 (small), write it first. For a box like 日 (sun), write the outside, then the inside, and close the bottom last. If the finished character looks the same, why does the order matter? First, it is shared. Teachers, textbooks, and the ministry's website all teach the same order, so everyone can learn from everyone else. Second, the order follows the flow of the brush. The ministry explains that the second stroke of 日 ends with a small hook because the brush is already turning toward the next stroke inside the box. Order also helps with structure. In 日, writing the middle stroke before the bottom line lets you see the space you are dividing, and the bottom line closes the box neatly at the end. In 三, writing from the top down lets you compare each line with the one above it, so you can make the middle one short and the bottom one long. Practice paper helps, too. A nine-square grid divides each square into nine smaller squares, like the character 井, so you can check where each stroke begins and ends. The 田 grid and the 米 grid do the same job. Long ago, children started school by tracing red characters in a copybook that began 上大人, 孔乙己, chosen because the characters have very few strokes. In this lesson, choose a rule and watch the 3D brush write its example character, with numbers showing the order. Try writing it backward, too, and notice that the result can look similar even though the order is not the standard one. Then play Guess the Next Stroke, and practice the six example characters on the practice pad.": "8630e2ef5c8c",
